She's said before that the village all acts like one big family. [pc.isBimbo
    |Is that, like, for real? Because it sounds awesome! A big happy family.
    |That seems... a little hard to believe sometimes.
]

"Families have arguments, too," Serena points out. "But yeah, I think it's still a fair way to describe us! A lot of people pair off and stay together, sure, but other adults kind of float between relationships or just, uh, fuck whomever. And every adult helps out with raising the kids in one way or another. You're not their mama, you're not really even their aunt, but you're still <b>involved</b>, you know?"

[pc.isBimbo
    |Hee hee. You like the lots of sex part.[pc.ora wyldelf
        | And the part about helping out with everybody else's babbus sounds like what wyld elves do!
    ] But, like, what if a new moo comes to the village?
    |[pc.ora wyldelf
        |Wyld elves do something similar; whether in a troupe in the woods or in a heartlander city, your[pc.ra wyldelf
            |
            | original
        ] people tend to help out a lot with others' kids. But still, i
        |I
    ]f the community's that tight-knit, is it welcoming to outsiders?
]

"There are bumpy spots[pc.isBimbo
    |, for sure, sweetie
    | when somebody leaves, or when a new person arrives
]," she admits. "But we <b>are</b> still able to handle changes. Sometimes people move on their own, and sometimes people get resettled to build the numbers back up in places that have... lost people. But for the most part, people stay with their tribe. You vote with the tribe, pay taxes with the tribe, serve in the legion with the tribe. It's possible to get your place on the rolls changed and switch tribes, usually through marriage, but it's just an awful process and most people don't bother.

"The other thing is the king's mercenaries, who sometimes convert their end-of-service bonuses into land grants or use them to build a home out in the village. Some of them even get citizenship grants for meritorious service! [serena.relationshipRange 1
    |After all, you've met little Matilda from my class;
    |One of the little girls in my class, Matilda, is a human through and through —
] both of her parents earned their citizenship in the auxiliaries for their heroism. But there are non-citizens who live out by us as well. I don't teach <b>their</b> children, but they still participate in village life, festivals, and so on. It works out okay."

[pc.isBimbo
    |So the family is a thing for sitty thingies! Mostly.
    |So the "big family" is mostly for the citizens.
]

Serena cocks her head bemusedly. "Well, yes. That's what citizenship is. Citizens have rights everybody else doesn't have, but we also have obligations non-citizens don't have. Take the village apothecarist, Zenon; he's a 'mander who used to fight for the king's hawks and spent his retirement pay to start a second career. He doesn't have to pay most of our taxes and his kids don't have to serve in the legion, but they also didn't get their letters in the village school. He's not a citizen, and neither is the rest of his family.

"With that said, the village is still the village, even for non-citizens. They dance in our festivals, trade with us, laugh with us, marry us... flee to Khor'minos with us when marauders come a-raiding. Zenon makes a lot of good coin, and counts everyone in the village a friend; his daughter Nyka — Nyktimene — is Lady Claritia's right hand, and she's much wealthier than I am... and a wonderful friend, to boot! And look at Vitra here, running a very popular tavern! Non-citizens are still Minoans. They're still us. They just don't have the same <b>legal</b> give and take as we do."

[pc.isBimbo
    |Oh, okay. So, like, how do you get to be a sitty thingy anyway, if you're not a moo?
    |Aha. Could a non-minotaur like old Zenon or his kids ever become a citizen?
]

She nods. "Granted for meritorious service, like I said. Some of the military heroes in the auxiliaries get the franchise because of their great deeds in battle or for long, loyal yeoman's work for the realm. There are even some villages that get block grants of citizenship: every free person in them, horns or no, becomes a citizen, usually after a war or for doing something to help the realm during a natural disaster. Most people you'll see around here without horns still aren't full citizens... but that's changing all the time."

