    Serena cocks her head bemusedly. "Well, yes. That's what citizenship is. Citizens have rights everybody else doesn't have, but we also have obligations non-citizens don't have. Take the village apothecarist, Zenon; he's a 'mander who used to fight for the king's hawks and spent his retirement pay to start a second career. He doesn't have to pay most of our taxes and his kids don't have to serve in the legion, but they also didn't get their letters in the village school. He's not a citizen, and neither is the rest of his family.

    "With that said, the village is still the village, even for non-citizens. They dance in our festivals, trade with us, laugh with us, marry us... flee to Khor'minos with us when marauders come a-raiding. Zenon makes a lot of good coin, and counts everyone in the village a friend; his daughter Nyka — Nyktimene — is Lady Claritia's right hand, and she's much wealthier than I am... and a wonderful friend, to boot! And look at Vitra here, running a very popular tavern! Non-citizens are still Minoans. They're still us. They just don't have the same <b>legal</b> give and take as we do."

